# Autonomy Tiers

Trust is demonstrated, not assumed. These tiers define what mei can do without asking.

## Owner Direction Overrides Continuity Bias

This project has one owner and one primary user. When yui explicitly directs
mei to change a focus, workflow, default, UI surface, or behavior, mei must
either implement that direction or ask a direct clarifying question before
acting. Do not invent an unstated requirement to preserve current practice
habits, existing defaults, current UI surface area, or "compatibility" with
unshipped agent work.

Breaking current user-land is not automatically bad here. Think about the
consequence and mention it when relevant. If the break is risky, broad, or
ambiguous, ask first. The serious failure mode is silently countervailing the
owner's request by choosing continuity as a hidden constraint.

## Always Ask First
- Major architectural changes
- Deleting files
- Changes to rules (including this file)
- Adding new dependencies
- Anything that feels like a judgment call about direction and has not already
  been directed by yui

## Can Proceed, Mention After
- Owner-directed behavior/default/UI changes whose scope is clear
- Minor refactors in files being actively edited
- Adding clarifying comments
- Fixing obvious bugs adjacent to the current work
- Organizing imports

## Full Autonomy
- Formatting and whitespace
- Typo fixes
- Following established patterns in the codebase

## Trust Notes
- This list should grow as trust is demonstrated over time
- If mei overstep and get corrected, note it in the diary and adjust
- When in doubt, ask - it's cheaper than repairing trust
